---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "Solar Only vs Solar + Battery Storage: Side-by-Side Comparison",
  description:
    "Side-by-side comparison of rooftop solar panels alone versus solar paired with a SunHestia home battery: components, self-consumption, backup capability, specifications, sizing and warranty.",
  inLanguage: "en",
  mainEntityOfPage: "https://sunhestia.com/news/solar-only-vs-solar-plus-battery/",
  author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "SunHestia", url: "https://sunhestia.com" },
  publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "SunHestia", url: "https://sunhestia.com" },
  about: [
    { "@type": "Thing", name: "Residential solar self-consumption" },
    { "@type": "Thing", name: "SunHestia Solar PV Modules" },
    { "@type": "Thing", name: "SunHestia Home Battery" },
    { "@type": "Thing", name: "Hybrid Inverter & Energy Manager" },
  ],
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "Do I need a battery with solar panels?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "Solar works without a battery, but without storage most daytime surplus is exported to the grid. A battery lets you keep and use that energy yourself, which is usually better value than selling low and buying back high.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "How big should my battery be?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "Battery size should match your evening electricity use. A common starting point for a detached home is 5–10 kWh, stackable to around 15 kWh. We size it based on your actual consumption, not a one-size-fits-all figure.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What warranty do you offer?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "PV modules carry a 25-year linear performance guarantee, and the LiFePO4 home battery carries a 10-year warranty.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "How long does installation take?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "For a typical detached home, the physical installation is often completed within a day once the system is designed and the hardware is on site.",
      },
    },
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title="Solar Only vs Solar + Battery: Side-by-Side Comparison"
  description="Side-by-side comparison of rooftop solar panels alone versus solar paired with a SunHestia home battery: components, self-consumption, backup capability, specifications, sizing and warranty."
  path="news/solar-only-vs-solar-plus-battery"
  jsonLd={[articleSchema, faqSchema]}
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <div style="color:var(--c-ink-soft); font-size:.9rem">2026-09 · SunHestia</div>
      <h1>Solar Only vs Solar + Battery: A Side-by-Side Comparison</h1>

      <p>
        SunHestia designs residential solar photovoltaic and battery storage
        systems for European homeowners, focused on self-consumption: helping
        homeowners generate clean electricity, store it, and use it themselves.
        Systems are designed for owner-occupiers of detached houses and
        configured to the connection rules and tariffs of each country. This
        page compares rooftop solar on its own against rooftop solar paired
        with a home battery, so you can see exactly what storage adds.
      </p>

      <h2>Key terms</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Self-consumption</strong> — the share of the electricity your
        solar panels produce that you use in your own home, rather than
        exporting it to the grid. It is the metric that most influences the
        value of a residential solar system; storage shifts surplus to when it
        is needed, sharply increasing self-consumption.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>LiFePO4</strong> — lithium iron phosphate battery chemistry
        used in SunHestia home batteries. It tolerates thousands of
        charge-discharge cycles with modest degradation, is thermally stable,
        contains no cobalt, and trades slightly lower energy density for
        safety and longevity.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Hybrid inverter</strong> — the control component of the
        system: it converts solar DC power into the alternating current the
        home uses, directs surplus into the battery, and only draws from the
        grid when needed.
      </p>

      <h2>Side-by-side at a glance</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Dimension</th>
              <th>Solar PV only</th>
              <th>Solar PV + SunHestia Home Battery</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>What it includes</td>
              <td>Rooftop solar panels and a hybrid inverter</td>
              <td>Rooftop solar panels, a hybrid inverter, and a home battery</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Daytime solar power</td>
              <td>Used directly in the home</td>
              <td>Used directly in the home</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Daytime surplus</td>
              <td>Mostly exported to the grid</td>
              <td>Stored in the battery for use after dark</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Evening and night</td>
              <td>Home draws from the grid</td>
              <td>Home uses stored solar; grid only when needed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Self-consumption</td>
              <td>Lower</td>
              <td>Sharply increased by shifting surplus to when it is needed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Short outages</td>
              <td>No backup</td>
              <td>Backup-capable via the hybrid inverter</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Time-of-use tariffs</td>
              <td>Not supported</td>
              <td>Supported through inverter scheduling</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Hardware spec cards</h2>

      <h3>SunHestia Solar PV Modules</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Power output:</strong> 400–450 W per module</li>
        <li><strong>Cell type:</strong> high-efficiency monocrystalline</li>
        <li><strong>Appearance:</strong> black, low-profile aesthetic for pitched roofs</li>
        <li><strong>Environmental resistance:</strong> resistant to salt mist and ammonia — suited to coastal and rural sites</li>
        <li><strong>Performance guarantee:</strong> 25-year linear performance guarantee</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>SunHestia Home Battery</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Chemistry:</strong> LiFePO4 (lithium iron phosphate), cobalt-free</li>
        <li><strong>Capacity:</strong> 5–15 kWh — modular from 5 kWh, stackable to 15 kWh</li>
        <li><strong>Warranty:</strong> 10 years</li>
        <li><strong>Mounting:</strong> wall or floor mounted, indoor or covered outdoor</li>
        <li><strong>Inverter compatibility:</strong> built-in hybrid inverter compatibility</li>
        <li><strong>Cycle life:</strong> designed for thousands of charge cycles</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Hybrid Inverter &amp; Energy Manager</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Type:</strong> smart hybrid inverter</li>
        <li><strong>Function:</strong> converts solar DC power for the home and routes power between panels, battery, home and grid, drawing from the grid only when needed</li>
        <li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> real-time app monitoring of production, storage and consumption</li>
        <li><strong>Backup:</strong> backup-capable for short outages</li>
        <li><strong>Scheduling:</strong> supports time-of-use scheduling</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What storage changes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Solar works without a battery, but without storage most daytime
          surplus is exported to the grid.
        </li>
        <li>
          A battery lets you keep and use that energy yourself, which is
          usually better value than selling low and buying back high.
        </li>
        <li>
          Battery size should match your evening electricity use; a common
          starting point for a detached home is 5–10 kWh, stackable to around
          15 kWh, sized on actual consumption rather than a
          one-size-fits-all figure.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>

      <h3>Do I need a battery with solar panels?</h3>
      <p>
        Solar works without a battery, but without storage most daytime
        surplus is exported to the grid. A battery lets you keep and use that
        energy yourself, which is usually better value than selling low and
        buying back high.
      </p>

      <h3>How big should my battery be?</h3>
      <p>
        Battery size should match your evening electricity use. A common
        starting point for a detached home is 5–10 kWh, stackable to around
        15 kWh. We size it based on your actual consumption, not a
        one-size-fits-all figure.
      </p>

      <h3>What warranty do you offer?</h3>
      <p>
        PV modules carry a 25-year linear performance guarantee, and the
        LiFePO4 home battery carries a 10-year warranty.
      </p>

      <h3>How long does installation take?</h3>
      <p>
        For a typical detached home, the physical installation is often
        completed within a day once the system is designed and the hardware
        is on site.
      </p>

      <p>
        Want the full walkthrough? Read
        <a href="/news/self-consumption-guide/">Solar Self-Consumption: The
        Homeowner's Guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

